    content: "Central Texas landscapes are known for their beautiful native trees and hardy shade varieties. In Travis County and Williamson County, TX, homeowners rely on trees not only for curb appeal but also for shade during long, hot summers.\nUnderstanding the most common local species \u2014 and how to properly care for them \u2014 helps protect your investment and prevent costly tree issues down the road.\nBelow are the top 10 tree species in Travis & Williamson County, TX and expert tips on how to care for each.\nLive Oak (Quercus virginiana)\nOne of the most iconic trees in Central Texas.\nWhy it\u2019s popular:\n\n Massive shade canopy\n Drought tolerant\n Long lifespan\n\nCare Tips:\n\n Prune properly to prevent weak limb structure\n Avoid excessive soil compaction around roots\n Inspect regularly for oak wilt\n Schedule professional pruning during safe seasons only\n\nCedar Elm\nA resilient native species commonly found in Austin-area neighborhoods.\nCare Tips:\n\n Remove dead limbs to prevent storm damage\n Monitor for aphids and leaf diseases\n Prune lightly to maintain structure\n\nTexas Red Oak (Spanish Oak)\nBeautiful fall color makes this a favorite in Williamson County.\nCare Tips:\n\n Watch closely for oak wilt\n Water during extreme drought\n Mulch around base to protect root zone\n\nPecan\nCommon in older properties and along creeks.\nCare Tips:\n\n Regular structural pruning\n Deep watering during dry periods\n Monitor for limb breakage (pecans can grow heavy branches)\n\nAshe Juniper (Cedar)\nOften misunderstood but very common in Central Texas.\nCare Tips:\n\n Remove selectively to reduce fire risk\n Thin overcrowded growth\n Monitor for drought stress\n\nBald Cypress\nFrequently planted near water features or low areas.\nCare Tips:\n\n Ensure adequate moisture\n Prune minimally\n Protect root flare from soil buildup\n\nCrape Myrtle\nExtremely popular ornamental tree in suburban areas.\nCare Tips:\n\n Avoid \u201ccrape murder\u201d (severe topping)\n Light pruning in late winter\n Ensure good airflow to prevent mildew\n\nMexican White Oak (Monterrey Oak)\nA fast-growing shade tree ideal for new developments.\nCare Tips:\n\n Monitor growth rate\n Structural pruning early in life\n Deep watering during establishment\n\nArizona Ash\nCommon in established neighborhoods but prone to structural issues.\nCare Tips:\n\n Inspect for trunk splitting\n Prune weak branch unions\n Consider removal if severe decline appears\n\nTexas Mountain Laurel\nA smaller ornamental tree known for fragrant purple blooms.\nCare Tips:\n\n Avoid overwatering\n Protect from mechanical damage\n Prune lightly to maintain shape\n\nTree Care Challenges in Travis & Williamson County\nCentral Texas trees face unique environmental stressors:\n\n Extreme summer heat\n Periodic drought\n Sudden severe storms\n Clay-heavy soil\n Occasional winter freezes\n\nProactive tree maintenance \u2014 including inspections, pruning, and soil management \u2014 helps reduce risk and extend the life of your trees.\nWhen to Call a Professional Tree Service\nYou should schedule a professional evaluation if you notice:\n\n Cracks in major limbs\n Leaning after heavy rain\n Dead or hanging branches\n Fungal growth on the trunk\n Sudden leaf loss outside normal seasons\n\nRoutine maintenance protects not only your trees, but also your home, driveway, and surrounding structures.\nFinal Tree Thoughts\nThe diverse tree population in Travis and Williamson County adds beauty and value to homes across Central Texas. Understanding the needs of these common species helps homeowners make smart maintenance decisions.\nIf you\u2019re unsure about the health or structural stability of a tree on your property, a professional inspection can provide peace of mind and prevent costly damage later.\nContact Brents Tree Service with any questions.",
